AUDIO: 'you ask for it. That\'s the Otter.ai replacement without the permanent pricing or the cloud copy of your meetings. The install really is one command. Docker run, port 1349, a data volume, done. It embeds Postgres 17 and'

[02:40] [scene:terminal]
VISUAL: Terminal showing a `docker run` command for SnapOtter.
AUDIO: 'Redis 8 inside the single container for the quick start. Log in with admin admin, change it, and start dropping files. For production, there\'s a proper three-container compose stack with the'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'same Postgres and Redis split out. And a nice security touch. Requests are served by a database role that can only read and write rows, while the owner role connects only at boot to migrate. It runs on AMD64 and ARM64, so'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Raspberry Pi and Apple Silicon both count. Whole thing is TypeScript, and the data directory is one volume, so backups are a copy job. How it sits'

[03:17] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Comparison slide: "Single-modality tools" vs "SnapOtter".
AUDIO: 'against the neighbors in their own framing. Sterling PDF is the beloved standard, but it stops at PDFs. Convert X covers conversions and stops there. Snap Otter\'s bet is breadth, all five'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'modalities, plus the editor, plus the AI Shelf in one stack. If you only ever touch PDFs, Sterling remains excellent. The moment your workflow crosses file types, the all-in-one starts earning its'

